11	/**
12	 * A foreign planet as the account's own espionage report saw it, never as it stands now.
13	 *
14	 * The raid planner and its battle screen used to load the live target: its real ships, defence and
15	 * stock. A player only knows what the last report said, so the AI was omniscient (never surprised by a
16	 * rebuilt wall or a fleet that came home) and timid at once (every defended target was simulated at
17	 * its true strength, so only empty planets passed and LIFE_FIGHTS sat at 9%). This builds a detached
18	 * copy of the planet row whose units and resources are the report's: the battle engine, the loot
19	 * formula and the launch ladder then answer "what does my report say", and the dispatch still meets
20	 * the live planet, which is where surprises, ninjas and lost fleets come from.
21	 *
22	 * The copy is never written: it is marked as not existing, so a stray save cannot reach the real row.
23	 * A report that could not see the fleet or the defence (too few probes) returns null: a player does
24	 * not raid blind, it sends more probes, which the spy planner's volley already does.
25	 */
26	class ReportedPlanet
27	{
